            <ArticleTitle>Outbreak of unexplained illness in a middle school--Washington, April 1994.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>6-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mass sociogenic illness (MSI) is the occurrence of a group of nonspecific physical symptoms for which no organic cause can be determined and that is transmitted among members of a group by &quot;line of sight.&quot; On April 22, 1994, the Snohomish (Washington) Health District (SHD) was notified of an outbreak of unexplained illness characterized by abrupt onset of nausea and headache among students at a middle school. This report summarizes the investigation of this outbreak by SHD, which determined that MSI was the most likely cause of the outbreak.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
